# Day 6 — EV Control System

## Overview

Day 6 focuses on the **Electric Vehicle (EV) Control Module** of the Real-Time EV Dashboard & ADAS Warning System.

The EV control module processes accelerator, brake, battery SOC, motor temperature, and drive-mode inputs to calculate the vehicle's operating parameters.

## Main Functions

* Accelerator pedal input processing
* Brake pedal input processing
* Motor torque calculation
* Vehicle speed monitoring
* Battery State of Charge (SOC) monitoring
* Motor temperature monitoring
* Drive-mode selection
* Regenerative braking
* Vehicle operating-state management

## Drive Modes

The system supports three drive modes:

| Drive Mode | Torque Factor |
| ---------- | ------------: |
| ECO        |         0.6 × |
| NORMAL     |         1.0 × |
| SPORT      |         1.3 × |

### ECO Mode

Reduces available motor torque to improve energy efficiency.

### NORMAL Mode

Provides normal vehicle performance with the standard torque output.

### SPORT Mode

Provides higher motor torque for improved acceleration and performance.

## Regenerative Braking

When the brake pedal is applied beyond the configured regeneration threshold, the system enters the regenerative-braking state.

Regenerative braking helps recover energy during deceleration by applying negative motor torque.

## EV Parameters

The EV controller maintains important parameters such as:

* Speed — km/h
* Motor torque — Nm
* Battery SOC — %
* Motor temperature — °C
* Accelerator pedal — %
* Brake pedal — %
* Estimated driving range — km
* Drive mode
* Vehicle state

## Vehicle States

The EV control logic uses different operating states:

* `STATE_PARKED`
* `STATE_READY`
* `STATE_DRIVING`
* `STATE_REGEN`
* `STATE_FAULT`

These states control how the vehicle responds to accelerator, brake, and fault conditions.

## Safety

The EV controller works together with the ADAS and fault-management modules.

If a critical fault occurs, the system can enter `STATE_FAULT` and disable motor torque to place the vehicle in a safe condition.

## Technologies Used

* STM32 Blue Pill
* STM32 HAL
* Embedded C
* ADC
* Timers
* GPIO
* EV control state machine
* Regenerative braking logic

## Learning Outcome

By completing Day 6, the EV control module demonstrates how an embedded controller can process vehicle inputs, select driving modes, calculate torque, manage regenerative braking, and maintain safe vehicle states.
